    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d601">

                <pb facs="0572" n="562" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg562"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.30 Asiatic Fleet/944</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Chief of the Division of Far
                        Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Adams</hi>)<anchor n="68"
                        xml:id="d601-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-09-10">September 10, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Reference Mr. Brandt’s attached memorandum of September 8<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d601-f2"/> in regard to possible withdrawal of Americans, including
                    marines, from China covering further memoranda on the same subject.</p>
                <p>There is offered for consideration the suggestion that FE be authorized to
                    discuss the matter orally with Captain Schuirmann of the Navy Department<anchor
                        n="70" xml:id="d601-f3"/> and to suggest to him that the Navy not make any
                    definite plans in regard to which information might become public at present,
                    but that the Navy Department keep in mind the question of affording Navy
                    transport accommodations for American civilians in China prior to or at the time
                    of the execution of any decision to withdraw the American Marines ashore in
                    China.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d601-f1" n="68" type="source" xml:id="d601-f1-content">
                <p>Initialed by the Chief of the Division (Hamilton) and the Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d601-f2" n="69" xml:id="d601-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; George L. Brandt was Chief of the Special Division.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d601-f3" n="70" xml:id="d601-f3-content">
                <p>Assistant in the Office of the Chief of Naval Operations.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
